media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T15:56:45Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Wo bas bijal ke set ki wait kar raha hai to exact revenge!", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T15:56:56Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "next", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T15:57:14Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "8.\tOn April 25, 1974, _Grândola, Vila Morena_, a banned folk song about solidarity and brotherhood started playing on the radio all over X. It was the prearranged signal for the left-wing Armed Forces Movement to begin what became one of the most unusual political revolutions of the 20th century: nearly bloodless, almost accidental in its gentleness, and appropriately named after a flower. Estado Novo, the fascist dictatorship established in 1933, the same year that Hitler came to power in Germany, had ruled X for 41 years. The revolution succeeded within hours. And then something strange and beautiful happened. Restaurant worker Celeste Caeiro who had been heading to work with flowers for the restaurant's anniversary celebration, came out into the street and began handing Ys to the soldiers. The soldiers — young men who had been fighting dirty colonial wars in Africa — put the flowers in the barrels of their rifles. The image spread across the country and the day’s events came to be known as the Y Revolution. ID X (5 points) and Y (10 points).", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T15:57:24Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Carnation", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T15:57:25Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Portugal", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T15:57:27Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Coup", "role": "chat", "is_